  "image_name": "3c24f5e8640de8de39392531175249ba.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two ultrafast solid-state spectroscopy ideas using band-structure sketches. On the left, high-order harmonic generation is shown in a crystal with valence and conduction bands versus crystal momentum. An intense ultrafast field first drives tunnelling of an electron from the valence band to the conduction band. The excited carrier then undergoes intraband acceleration as it moves along the conduction band, which can emit an intraband harmonic beam. A second pathway is interband recombination, where the electron returns to the valence band and emits another harmonic beam. The image emphasizes that HHG in solids contains both intraband motion and interband electron–hole recombination.\n\nOn the right, attosecond transient spectroscopy uses an HHG-generated extreme-ultraviolet pulse to probe the material. The XUV pulse is absorbed from a lower state into the conduction band, while a delayed pump beam excites carriers and initiates dynamics in the bands. The comparison highlights a role reversal: in panel a, HHG is generated by strong-field carrier motion inside the solid, whereas in panel b, HHG serves as the probing light source for tracking ultrafast electronic dynamics. Some fine labels are small, but this overall sequence is clear.",
